hough Cook, Inside Elections and Sabato still say Tossup", "Mendoza has outraised incumbent Ciscomani and holds strong cash-on-hand", "No usable general-election polls; the June D-sponsored poll showed only a 2-point race"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a favorable Democratic environment in an even-PVI seat, and Mendoza's fundraising edge plus markets pricing her near 80% justify a Lean D flip call. Ciscomani's incumbency and the thin, dated, sponsor-leaning polling keep the margin modest and uncertainty wide.", "watch": "Any fresh independent polling or a shift in the national House environment could move this back toward a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Juan Ciscomani (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 6th District is an even-PVI swing seat where first-term Republican Juan Ciscomani faces Democrat JoAnna Mendoza in a rematch-style contest. Mendoza has raised about $7.4 million to Ciscomani's $6.2 million and holds $3.3 million on hand, and she has drawn national attention including a Buttigieg rally. The two met in a single September debate that covered the economy and other core issues.\n\nMost forecasters now rate the seat a Democratic flip, though Cook, Inside Elections and Sabato's Crystal Ball still call it a Tossup. Prediction markets on Kalshi, Polymarket and PredictIt price Mendoza between 74% and 82%. With no reliable general-election polling available, the race rests on fundamentals, money and expert consensus, all of which tilt Democratic but leave real room for an incumbent upset.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8163, "margin": 5.47}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8157, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8181, "margin": 5.5}]}